    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old male with a 50-pack-year smoking history presents with a painless, white plaque on his right true vocal fold. What is the clinical term for this finding, and what does it imply histologically?', '["Leukoplakia; it always implies invasive cancer", "Erythroplakia; it implies benign hyperkeratosis", "Leukoplakia; it is a purely clinical descriptive term that requires biopsy to rule out dysplasia or carcinoma", "Pachydermia; it implies acid reflux", "Candidiasis; it implies fungal infection"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Leukoplakia translates to "white patch." It is a clinical description, not a histologic diagnosis. A biopsy is mandatory to determine if it is benign hyperkeratosis, dysplasia, or invasive squamous cell carcinoma (SCC).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which clinical finding is statistically more concerning for harboring severe dysplasia or invasive carcinoma: a pure white plaque (leukoplakia) or a red, velvety plaque (erythroplakia) on the vocal fold?', '["Leukoplakia", "Erythroplakia", "They carry the exact same risk", "Neither are concerning", "Only if they are both present together"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Erythroplakia (a red, velvety patch) indicates a lack of surface keratin and a rich vascular supply, which is highly correlated with severe dysplasia or carcinoma in situ (CIS) compared to purely white lesions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'If a vocal fold biopsy returns as "Severe Dysplasia" or "Carcinoma in Situ (CIS)," what is the most appropriate management?', '["Observation only", "Total laryngectomy", "Complete micro-laryngeal surgical excision of the lesion (e.g., using a CO2 laser) while preserving the underlying vocal ligament, and strict smoking cessation", "Systemic chemotherapy", "Antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 2, 'CIS represents cancer cells that have not yet breached the basement membrane. It must be completely removed to prevent progression to invasive cancer, but conservative phonomicrosurgery can usually preserve the voice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most significant and well-established risk factor for developing Squamous Cell Carcinoma of the larynx?', '["Alcohol consumption alone", "Chronic acid reflux", "Heavy, chronic tobacco use (smoking)", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV) infection", "Vocal abuse/yelling"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Smoking is the primary driver of laryngeal SCC. The combination of heavy smoking and heavy alcohol consumption has a synergistic (multiplying) effect, dramatically increasing the risk.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Unlike oropharyngeal cancer, what role does Human Papillomavirus (HPV) play in the majority of LARYNGEAL squamous cell carcinomas?', '["It causes 90% of them", "It causes laryngeal cancer only in children", "It plays a very minor role; the vast majority of laryngeal SCCs are driven by tobacco and alcohol, not HPV", "It causes only subglottic cancers", "It prevents laryngeal cancer"]'::jsonb, 2, 'While HPV (specifically HPV-16) is the leading cause of tonsil and base of tongue (oropharyngeal) cancers, it causes less than 5-10% of laryngeal cancers.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which anatomical subsite of the larynx is the MOST COMMON location for laryngeal cancer?', '["Supraglottis", "Glottis (true vocal folds)", "Subglottis", "Laryngeal ventricle", "Arytenoid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Glottic cancer accounts for approximately 60-65% of all laryngeal cancers, followed by supraglottic (30-35%). Primary subglottic cancer is very rare (<5%).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why do T1 Glottic Squamous Cell Carcinomas have an exceptionally high cure rate (>90%)?', '["They are benign tumors", "They present very early because even a tiny tumor on the vibrating edge causes noticeable hoarseness, prompting the patient to seek care", "They grow extremely slowly", "They respond to antibiotics", "They fall off on their own"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The true vocal folds are highly sensitive to any change in mass. A 1mm tumor causes hoarseness, acting as an early warning alarm before the tumor has a chance to grow or spread.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In addition to early presentation, what anatomical feature of the glottis contributes to the low rate of regional lymph node metastasis in early glottic cancer?', '["It is covered in thick cartilage", "The true vocal folds have virtually no capillary lymphatic drainage system", "It drains directly into the bloodstream", "The lymph nodes in the neck are immune to it", "The tumor produces anti-lymphatic factors"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because the glottis is practically devoid of lymphatics, early tumors (T1, T2) remain localized to the vocal fold. Lymph node metastasis is extremely rare (<5%) unless the tumor grows off the vocal fold into the supraglottis or subglottis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What specifically defines a T1 glottic cancer in the TNM staging system?', '["It is larger than 2 cm", "It involves the lymph nodes", "The tumor is confined to the true vocal fold(s) and the vocal folds have normal mobility", "It invades the thyroid cartilage", "It causes vocal fold paralysis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'T1 glottic tumors are confined to the glottis with normal cord mobility. If the cord becomes fixed (paralyzed), it is automatically upstaged to T3, indicating deeper muscle invasion.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What are the two standard, equally effective treatment options for a patient with an early (T1 or T2) glottic squamous cell carcinoma?', '["Total laryngectomy OR chemotherapy", "Definitive radiation therapy OR transoral laser microsurgery (TOLM)", "Chemotherapy OR immunotherapy", "Observation OR antibiotics", "Tracheostomy OR vocal cord stripping"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both radiation and transoral laser surgery aim for single-modality cure while preserving the voice. The choice depends on patient preference, tumor exact location, and surgeon expertise. Total laryngectomy is overtreatment for early disease.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 65-year-old male with a history of smoking presents with a 3-month history of a "hot potato" voice, severe pain when swallowing (odynophagia), and right-sided ear pain, though his ear exam is completely normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Otitis media", "Vocal fold nodule", "Supraglottic squamous cell carcinoma", "LPR", "Croup"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A muffled voice, dysphagia, and referred otalgia (ear pain) in a smoker strongly suggest a supraglottic tumor (e.g., epiglottis or aryepiglottic fold).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why does a patient with a supraglottic cancer often complain of severe ear pain (referred otalgia)?', '["The tumor directly invades the ear canal", "Sensory innervation of the supraglottis (via CN X) shares central brainstem pathways with sensory nerves of the ear (CN IX, X), causing the brain to misinterpret the throat pain as ear pain", "The tumor blocks the Eustachian tube", "It causes an ear infection", "The tumor secretes a toxin"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Referred otalgia is a classic "red flag" symptom for malignancies of the pharynx or supraglottis. The Vagus nerve supplies both the larynx and the external ear canal (Arnold''s nerve).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Why do Supraglottic cancers frequently present at a much more advanced stage with regional lymph node metastases compared to Glottic cancers?', '["They grow twice as fast", "The supraglottis has a rich, bilateral lymphatic network, and tumors here do not affect the voice until they are very large", "They are painless until they reach the brain", "They are highly resistant to the immune system", "They only occur in immunocompromised patients"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The "silent" nature of the supraglottis means tumors can grow large before causing symptoms, and the rich lymphatics allow early spread to the neck nodes (up to 40-50% have nodal metastasis at presentation).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'When treating a primary supraglottic squamous cell carcinoma, what is the mandatory approach to the lymph nodes of the neck?', '["They can be safely ignored", "Only the ipsilateral neck needs treatment", "Both sides of the neck (bilateral neck dissection or bilateral radiation) must be treated because supraglottic tumors frequently drain bilaterally", "Only the submandibular nodes are removed", "Only treat nodes if they are palpable"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Because of the rich, crossing lymphatic drainage of the supraglottis, microscopic disease is assumed to be present in both sides of the neck, requiring bilateral elective treatment.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female smoker presents to the ED with severe dyspnea and biphasic stridor. She ignored a mild hoarseness for the past 6 months. Endoscopy reveals a large, smooth, submucosal-appearing mass filling the subglottis and extending into the trachea. What is the diagnosis?', '["Glottic cancer", "Primary Subglottic squamous cell carcinoma", "Vocal fold polyp", "Reinke''s edema", "Epiglottitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Primary subglottic cancers are rare, grow silently, and almost always present late with airway obstruction (stridor) requiring emergency airway management.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which specific lymph node basin is uniquely at high risk for metastasis from a Subglottic carcinoma?', '["Submandibular nodes (Level I)", "Paratracheal, pretracheal (Delphian node), and superior mediastinal nodes (Level VI)", "Occipital nodes", "Post-auricular nodes", "Retropharyngeal nodes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While supraglottic tumors drain up to the jugular nodes, subglottic tumors drain down into the central neck and the mediastinum, following the path of the trachea.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What clinical finding on physical exam or laryngoscopy automatically upstages a laryngeal cancer to at least a T3 stage?', '["Vocal fold fixation (paralysis) caused by tumor invading the paraglottic space or the vocalis muscle", "A tumor larger than 1 cm", "A tumor on the epiglottis", "Hoarseness", "A palpable neck node"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Vocal cord fixation is a critical prognostic sign. It indicates deep muscle or nerve invasion and moves the tumor into the "advanced" category (T3).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What defines a T4 laryngeal cancer?', '["Tumor invading the vocalis muscle", "Tumor confined to the supraglottis", "Tumor invading through the thyroid cartilage and extending into the soft tissues of the neck, or invading the deep tongue root/esophagus", "Tumor with a single lymph node metastasis", "Tumor causing hoarseness"]'::jsonb, 2, 'T4 indicates massive local invasion escaping the cartilaginous framework of the larynx. These often require total laryngectomy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'For advanced laryngeal cancer (T3, T4), what are the two main treatment paradigms considered standard of care?', '["Chemotherapy alone", "Organ preservation therapy (concurrent Chemoradiation) OR primary surgical resection (Total Laryngectomy) followed by radiation", "Transoral laser microsurgery only", "Hospice care", "Antibiotics and steroids"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The landmark VA Laryngeal Cancer Study proved that chemoradiation can preserve the larynx in many advanced cases with survival rates equal to total laryngectomy. Surgery is reserved for salvage or massive destructive tumors (T4a).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoes a Total Laryngectomy for advanced laryngeal cancer. Which of the following statements about their post-operative airway is true?', '["They will breathe through their mouth and nose normally", "The trachea is brought out and sutured directly to the skin of the neck (a permanent tracheostoma); they will breathe exclusively through this hole in their neck for the rest of their life", "They require a permanent endotracheal tube", "They will breathe through a temporary tracheostomy that will eventually be closed", "They will only breathe out of their nose"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In a total laryngectomy, the entire larynx is removed. The airway is completely and permanently separated from the digestive tract.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'After a Total Laryngectomy, can the patient ever aspirate food or liquid into their lungs during normal swallowing?', '["Yes, frequently", "No, it is anatomically impossible because the trachea and the esophagus are completely separated", "Only if they eat too fast", "Only liquids", "Yes, because the vocal cords are gone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The permanent separation of the airway from the pharynx means the patient can swallow safely without any risk of aspiration into the lungs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most common and generally most successful method of voice restoration for a patient who has undergone a Total Laryngectomy?', '["Esophageal speech (swallowing air and burping it back up)", "An Electrolarynx (a vibrating device held to the neck)", "Tracheoesophageal Puncture (TEP) with a one-way voice prosthesis", "A laryngeal transplant", "Learning sign language"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A TEP creates a small fistula between the trachea and esophagus. The patient occludes their stoma, forcing exhaled lung air through the one-way valve into the esophagus, causing the pharyngeal mucosa to vibrate and produce a fluent, natural-sounding voice.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How does an Electrolarynx work for a post-laryngectomy patient?', '["It is surgically implanted in the brain", "It is a battery-powered vibrating device held against the neck or cheek; the vibrations are transmitted into the mouth where the tongue and lips articulate them into robotic-sounding speech", "It blows air into the stomach", "It uses a computer to read the patient''s mind", "It functions like a megaphone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'It provides a mechanical sound source to replace the missing vocal folds. It is easy to learn but sounds very robotic and mechanical.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following is a strict contraindication to performing a partial, voice-preserving laryngectomy (e.g., Supracricoid Laryngectomy) instead of a Total Laryngectomy?', '["The patient has a T1 tumor", "The patient has poor pulmonary function (e.g., severe COPD) and cannot tolerate the inevitable mild aspiration that occurs during the recovery phase", "The patient is a singer", "The patient is female", "The patient is over 50"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Partial laryngectomies reconstruct a smaller, less protective larynx. Patients MUST have excellent lung reserves to cough up aspirated food/liquids while they relearn to swallow, otherwise they will die of aspiration pneumonia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with laryngeal cancer has a tumor that involves the anterior commissure and extends subglottically by 15 mm. Why is this specific tumor extension highly concerning and often an indication for total laryngectomy rather than radiation?', '["It makes the voice too high pitched", "The subglottis is poorly vascularized, and tumor here readily invades the cricoid cartilage and escapes the larynx; radiation often fails in this hypoxic, cartilaginous environment", "It causes immediate death", "It spreads to the brain", "It prevents eating"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Subglottic extension is a poor prognostic factor. Tumors quickly invade the cricothyroid membrane and cricoid cartilage, requiring extensive surgical resection.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary purpose of a "neck dissection" in the surgical treatment of laryngeal cancer?', '["To remove the thyroid gland", "To remove the lymph nodes of the neck that are at risk for harboring microscopic or macroscopic metastatic cancer", "To remove the jugular vein", "To relieve pain", "To improve the voice"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A neck dissection clears the lymphatic basins (Levels II, III, IV for laryngeal cancer) to prevent or treat regional recurrence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient receives concurrent chemoradiation for a T3 glottic cancer. One year later, he presents with severe hoarseness and a fixed vocal cord. Biopsy confirms recurrent cancer. What is the only curative treatment option remaining?', '["More radiation", "More chemotherapy", "Salvage Total Laryngectomy", "Laser microsurgery", "Botox"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Once primary chemoradiation fails, the tissue is too damaged for further radiation or conservative surgery. A salvage total laryngectomy is required to remove the recurrence and prevent fatal complications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is a major complication of performing a Salvage Total Laryngectomy on heavily irradiated tissue?', '["The voice will be too loud", "A high risk of Pharyngocutaneous Fistula (saliva leaking from the pharynx through the neck skin) due to poor wound healing and poor blood supply", "Immediate heart failure", "Vocal cord paralysis", "Blindness"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Irradiated tissue is ischemic and heals poorly. The surgical closure of the pharynx often breaks down, requiring complex flap reconstruction (e.g., pectoralis major flap) to close the hole.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'In the context of airway management, what defines a "secure" airway?', '["An oxygen mask on the face", "A cuffed tube placed in the trachea (e.g., an endotracheal tube or a tracheostomy tube) protecting the lungs from aspiration", "A nasal cannula", "A laryngeal mask airway (LMA)", "A clear mouth"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A secure airway specifically means the trachea is intubated with a device that has an inflated balloon (cuff) to prevent blood, vomit, or secretions from entering the lungs.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is scheduled for an elective tracheostomy. At what cartilaginous level should the surgeon make the incision into the trachea?', '["Through the cricothyroid membrane", "Above the cricoid cartilage", "Between the 2nd and 3rd, or 3rd and 4th tracheal rings", "Between the 6th and 7th tracheal rings", "Through the thyroid cartilage"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is the standard placement for a surgical tracheostomy, ensuring it is low enough to avoid cricoid injury and high enough to avoid the innominate artery.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the most critical initial step if a newly placed tracheostomy tube (less than 3 days old) accidentally dislodges or falls out?', '["Attempt to blindly jam it back into the hole", "Call for help, oxygenate from above (face mask ventilation), and prepare for oral intubation; do NOT blindly force the tube back in as you may create a false passage into the mediastinum", "Ignore it", "Give antibiotics", "Start CPR"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A fresh tracheostomy tract is not mature. Blindly pushing a tube often slides it into the soft tissues of the neck instead of the trachea, causing instant, fatal airway loss.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'How long does it typically take for a tracheostomy tract to mature (form a scarred, stable tunnel from the skin to the trachea) making tube changes relatively safe?', '["1 hour", "12 hours", "5-7 days", "1 month", "6 months"]'::jsonb, 2, 'It takes about a week for the tract to mature. The first tube change is usually performed by the surgical team around day 5-7.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the purpose of the "inner cannula" of a tracheostomy tube?', '["It makes the tube look better", "It can be quickly removed and cleaned if it becomes plugged with thick mucus, instantly restoring a patent airway without having to remove the entire outer tube", "It administers medication", "It inflates the cuff", "It prevents speaking"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Mucous plugging is a major risk. A dual-cannula system allows the caregiver to simply pull out the inner sleeve to clear a blockage instantly.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a tracheostomy wants to speak. The speech therapist places a Passy-Muir speaking valve on the tube. What absolutely MUST be done to the tracheostomy tube before placing the valve?', '["The tube must be removed", "The cuff (balloon) of the tracheostomy tube MUST be completely DEFLATED", "The cuff must be over-inflated", "The inner cannula must be glued in", "The patient must be sedated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A speaking valve is a one-way valve that lets air IN through the tube, but forces air OUT through the vocal cords to make sound. If the cuff is inflated, the patient cannot exhale and will suffocate immediately.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a tracheostomy presents with severe dyspnea. You suspect a mucous plug. What is the correct sequence of actions?', '["Give inhalers, then steroids", "Remove the speaking valve, remove the inner cannula, suction the tube, and if still obstructed, deflate the cuff and remove the entire tracheostomy tube while preparing to intubate from above", "Push the tube deeper", "Inflate the cuff", "Start chest compressions"]'::jsonb, 1, 'You systematically remove the possible obstructions. If the tube itself is hopelessly blocked, it must be removed to save the patient''s life.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the definition of "stridor"?', '["A cough", "A high-pitched, noisy breathing sound caused by turbulent airflow through a narrowed upper airway (larynx or trachea)", "A rattling sound in the chest", "A wheeze from asthma", "A sneeze"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Stridor is the hallmark symptom of upper airway obstruction and is a clinical sign that the airway is critically narrowed.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During evaluation of a patient with stridor, what does INSPIRATORY stridor typically indicate?', '["Asthma", "An obstruction located ABOVE the true vocal folds (supraglottis or pharynx) such as Laryngomalacia or Epiglottitis", "An obstruction in the lungs", "A foreign body in the right mainstem bronchus", "A tracheal tumor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Inspiratory stridor localizes the lesion high in the airway. Expiratory stridor localizes it low (intrathoracic trachea/bronchi). Biphasic stridor localizes it to the glottis or subglottis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old male presents with a hoarse, breathy voice. Laryngoscopy reveals a large, unilateral mass on the true vocal fold causing incomplete glottic closure. What acoustic measure on voice analysis will be significantly elevated in this patient?', '["Fundamental frequency", "Maximum phonation time", "Jitter and Shimmer (measures of cycle-to-cycle frequency and amplitude instability caused by the irregular mass)", "Vocal range", "Loudness"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A mass disrupts the smooth, periodic vibration of the vocal folds, causing the acoustic signal to become noisy and irregular, measured as elevated jitter and shimmer.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the "Maximum Phonation Time" (MPT)?', '["How loud a patient can yell", "The longest time a patient can sustain a vowel sound (e.g., ''ah'') on a single deep breath; it evaluates respiratory capacity and glottic efficiency", "How fast they can speak", "How high they can sing", "How long they can hold their breath without making a sound"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A normal MPT is 15-25 seconds. A breathy voice (glottic insufficiency) allows air to escape rapidly, severely reducing the MPT to <5-10 seconds.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which of the following describes a "Cordectomy"?', '["Removal of the spinal cord", "Surgical removal of a portion of, or the entire, true vocal fold, often performed via laser for early glottic cancer", "Removal of the epiglottis", "Removal of the entire larynx", "Injecting the vocal cord"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A cordectomy excises the cancerous vocal fold tissue. Depending on the extent (Type I to Type VI), it results in varying degrees of permanent hoarseness.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient presents to the clinic with severe hoarseness. He states he has been treating his "asthma" with a steroid inhaler for years. Endoscopy reveals a white, exophytic mass on the vocal cord. What critical diagnostic mistake was likely made years ago?', '["He was given the wrong asthma medication", "He was never evaluated by an ENT with laryngoscopy to differentiate true asthma (lower airway) from an obstructing laryngeal tumor (upper airway) presenting with wheezing/stridor", "He didn''t use a spacer", "He didn''t take antibiotics", "He didn''t get a chest X-ray"]'::jsonb, 1, 'It is a classic pitfall to misdiagnose stridor from a slow-growing airway tumor or subglottic stenosis as "adult-onset asthma" without ever visualizing the larynx.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the classic histological appearance of a typical laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma?', '["Spindle cells in a fascicular pattern", "Nests of malignant squamous epithelial cells invading the stroma, often with keratin pearls and intercellular bridges", "Small round blue cells", "Gland-forming cells with mucin production", "Granular cells staining S-100 positive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Keratin pearls and intercellular bridges are the defining histologic hallmarks of well-to-moderately differentiated squamous cell carcinoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a suspected laryngeal cancer is scheduled for a "Panendoscopy." What does this procedure entail?', '["A CT scan of the whole body", "Direct laryngoscopy, bronchoscopy, and esophagoscopy under general anesthesia to map the primary tumor and rule out synchronous second primary tumors in the aerodigestive tract", "An MRI of the brain", "An awake flexible scope in the office", "A surgical incision in the neck"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because smokers/drinkers have "field cancerization" of their entire upper airway and digestive tract, they have a 5-10% risk of having a second, separate cancer in the lungs or esophagus at the time of diagnosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Which complication is most feared following a massive radiation dose to the larynx for advanced cancer, resulting in severe pain, foul breath, and exposed, dead cartilage?', '["Vocal nodules", "Chondroradionecrosis of the larynx", "Reinke''s edema", "Laryngocele", "Granuloma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Radiation kills the blood supply to the laryngeal cartilages. If infection sets in, the cartilage dies and rots (chondroradionecrosis), often requiring a salvage total laryngectomy to stop the severe pain and aspiration.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the role of a PET-CT scan in the workup of a new advanced laryngeal cancer?', '["To diagnose vocal cord paralysis", "To accurately stage the neck for regional lymph node metastasis and evaluate the entire body for distant metastasis (e.g., lungs, bone, liver)", "To treat the cancer", "To look for vocal nodules", "To guide Botox injections"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A PET-CT highlights metabolically active (glucose-consuming) cancer cells. It is critical for M-staging (distant metastasis) before embarking on aggressive curative treatments.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a massive T4 laryngeal cancer invading the skin of the neck is deemed unresectable and has widespread lung metastases. What is the primary goal of treatment at this stage?', '["Curative surgery", "Curative radiation", "Palliative care focusing on securing the airway (tracheostomy), pain control, and maintaining nutritional access (G-tube)", "Aggressive physical therapy", "Voice therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In stage IVC (distant metastasis) or unresectable disease, the goal shifts from cure to maximizing quality of life, preventing asphyxiation, and managing pain.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a laryngectomy, the surgeon preserves the thyroid gland if possible, but must often remove the parathyroid glands if they are involved with tumor. What post-operative blood abnormality must be monitored closely?', '["Hyperglycemia", "Severe Hypocalcemia (causing tetany, perioral numbness, and Chvostek''s sign)", "Hyperkalemia", "Hyponatremia", "High cholesterol"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Loss of parathyroid hormone leads to a rapid drop in serum calcium, requiring immediate IV calcium supplementation to prevent cardiac arrhythmias and seizures.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a total laryngectomy presents to the ED in respiratory arrest. Where should the resuscitation team place the bag-valve mask to provide oxygen?', '["Over the nose and mouth", "Over the nose only", "Directly over the permanent tracheostoma in the neck (using a pediatric mask or connecting directly to the stoma tube)", "Over the mouth only", "Over the chest"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The patient has no connection between their mouth/nose and their lungs. All rescue breathing MUST be delivered directly into the neck stoma.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'What is the primary anatomical boundary that separates the "supraglottis" from the "glottis"?', '["The hyoid bone", "The cricoid cartilage", "The laryngeal ventricle (specifically, the apex of the ventricle between the false and true vocal folds)", "The epiglottis", "The vocal processes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The apex of the laryngeal ventricle separates the supraglottic region (false cords, epiglottis, aryepiglottic folds) from the glottic region (true vocal folds and anterior/posterior commissures).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old female presents with hoarseness. Laryngoscopy reveals a small, firm, unilateral lesion on the medial edge of the true vocal fold. She is a singer. To minimize the risk of permanent scarring and voice loss, what specific surgical technique should be employed if excision is necessary?', '["Total vocal cord stripping", "Cold-instrument phonomicrosurgery using the microflap technique to preserve the superficial lamina propria", "Extensive laser vaporization", "Cauterization of the vocal fold", "Total laryngectomy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Preserving the delicate layers of the vocal fold, specifically Reinke''s space, is the paramount rule of phonomicrosurgery to maintain the mucosal wave and a normal voice.')
